h  advanced planning lots of legal  documents and agreements before moving  in and monthly house meetings plus two  social meetings every week when  schedules permit those are a bit more  casual we do them over dinner  we cook each other dinner one night a  week and those are kind of spaces to  come together and chat these four  millennial buyers whose annual pay  ranges from 60 to 120 000  are part of a growing trend in the most  expensive urban centers toronto-based  co-ownership company arboro has seen a  nearly 400 percent surge in interest in  their services this year compared with  the end of last year some trying to get  ahead of rising interest rates others  looking to get into a market they can't  access on their own when they learn  about it they they feel like  where has this been kind of all my life  these co-owners are celebrating one year  since they purchased their home together  and they have this advice for people  considering this path it takes time and  it takes it does take effort but i think  there's a huge payoff  be honest with yourself first and what  your needs are because in co-housing it  can get really easy if that's something  you're thinking about to people please  or you know go with the group you know  it increases affordability it increases  options for them  and it helps build community and i think  leads to a to a richer and more  fulfilling life  an increasingly popular alternative for  those feeling priced out of the housing  market ann caviola global news toronto  the long road to reconciliation next  what a former chief of the assembly of  first nations hopes to achieve on his  return to rome  a great deal is riding on the meetings  between indigenous leaders and pope  francis this week residential school  survivors and their families have waited  years for an apology from the catholic  church one of those survivors is phil  fontaine the former national chief of  the assembly of first nations  reconciliation healing and forgive